Added `identitydemo`, a sibling package holding the federation demo stack, together with `Dockerfile.demo` and a two-container compose stack. It is never published: the wheel ships `src/pas` and nothing else, so the demo profiles, the fixed client secret and the example users stay out of what `pip install pas.plugins.identity` gives you. One package rather than two, and one image run twice, because the difference between an identity provider and a relying party here is two GenericSetup profiles — `identitydemo:idp` and `identitydemo:rp` — not two bodies of code.

uv workspaces were the obvious mechanism and are the wrong one: they belong to uv's managed-project interface, which this repository opts out of with `managed = false` in favour of mxdev against the Plone constraints. An `mx.ini` source was the next attempt and is also wrong: `Dockerfile` copies the whole tree and runs mxdev over it, so a source section there would put these profiles into the production image's `portal_setup`. The install is explicit at each of the two places that want it, `make install-demo` and `Dockerfile.demo`, and the demo is unreachable from the production image. The distribution name matches the import package exactly, because `plone.autoinclude` derives a module name from the distribution and would otherwise fail Zope startup looking for `identity_demo`.

Both profiles refuse to install unless `IDENTITY_DEMO` is set, and say so rather than quietly doing nothing. The credentials are fixed literals so that two containers which never speak to each other at install time can still agree on them, which is exactly the property that makes applying either profile to a real site a bad afternoon. Where a literal is stated twice — once in `identitydemo.settings` for the Python handlers, once in profile XML for GenericSetup — a test asserts the two agree, because nothing else would. @ericof
